{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgiCcHyTr8KMxvNcSwYH9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've built **Olympiyikes!** (\"Faster. Higher. Yikes.\"), an over-the-top arcade Summer + Winter Games, as `Olympiyikes-OlympicGames-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-074439.html` in the folder root, next to `exit-interview.json`. The final run of `node tests/run.mjs` gave **463 checks, 0 failed**: the headless suites plus the browser suites, including the real AI Hub harness at 1 player, 2 players, 4 players with latency and packet loss, and host-leave.\n\n**What's in it**\n- **12 events:** 100m dash, javelin, 50m freestyle, 10m diving, archery, weightlifting, ski jump, downhill, two-man bobsled, figure skating, curling final and ice hockey final. Each has CPU rivals, its own turbo, its yikes wipeouts, a laurel, 6+ live-tunable values, commentary, music, sound effects and an attract-mode segment.\n- **The Games campaign:** opening ceremony with a parade and a playable cauldron throw, 12 days alternating summer and winter, story beats with the rival Max Overdrive, results, top-5 initials, medal ceremonies with a generated anthem per nation, the medal table, qualifying with torches and GAMES OVER/CONTINUE, autosave and Continue, the closing ceremony, endings by final rank, and credits.\n- **Other modes:** Quick Games (4-event playlists), Single Event with a practice option, Records/Laurels/Cabinet, How to Play, Settings, and About & Credits.\n- **Multiplayer and online:** 1–4 players on one machine. AI Hub \"Hub Heats\" in ghost mode: three dealt events, the other players shown as ghosts, points per event.\n- **Dev mode** (from the pause menu, or the ` key): live sliders with reset to defaults, cheats (autopilot, win/lose now, skip to phase), jumps to any screen, view and data tools.\n- **Attract mode** starts after 10 s idle on any menu screen. Touch-only devices get a notice with a Settings button.\n\n**Where I departed from the plan or cut corners**\n- **Curling** uses its own small stone physics instead of the engine's physics module. Its CPU skip still plans each shot by simulating 108 candidates.\n- **The YIKES dive** needs 9 half-somersaults at a faster spin instead of the plan's 10, because 10 can't be done before hitting the water.\n- **Bobsled qualifying** is loosened to 94/88/84.5 s (Easy/Normal/Hard). Even a near-perfect bot couldn't make the plan's 86 s on Normal.\n- **Downhill and bobsled ghosts** (in hub sessions and the leader's run) are simple translucent shapes, not full athletes.\n- **The autopilot:** buttons go through the real input path, but it feeds its analog stick value directly instead of through keys. The weightlifting and diving bots set their menu choice directly.\n- **Balance is only tested with bots.** The playthrough proves every screen and event works, but nobody has played it with hands on a pad, and no one has listened to the gibberish voices or the music.\n- **Text on the engine's settings sheet** (the sheet itself, not this game's own screens) is still on the small side at 1080p after my scaling.\n\n---\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 07:44:39\n- **Source:** The complete Summer and Winter Olympic Games (over-the-top arcade style)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:**\n  - Genre: arcade multi-event sports with a short campaign, local party play and online ghost heats.\n  - Perspective, using the engine's views:\n    - 2.5D side-on lanes: 100m, javelin, 50m freestyle, weightlifting\n    - side view: diving, ski jump, the archer's inset\n    - target close-up: archery\n    - pseudo-3D from behind (the engine's road module): downhill, bobsled\n    - three-quarter: figure skating, ice hockey\n    - top-down: curling\n- **Tech:**\n  - my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff, full engine (road and rhythm modules used), modified in 6 marked places\n  - AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0, unmodified\n  - plain JavaScript, no libraries\n  - Node 24 + Playwright 1.63.0 (Chromium) for tests\n  - one self-contained HTML file\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native PS1-era pixel art with dressed HD Humanoid rigs. New art drawn in code: flags, 12 pictograms, the Yikes Flame emblem, 10 venues, crowds, jumbotrons, equipment and the mascot Yikesy.\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural Humanoid and Blob rigs, plus a new pose layer (on an engine pose hook with whole-body rotation) for sprint blocks, javelin throws, swimming, dives, archery, lifts, ski flight, bobsled, skating, sweeping and hockey.\n- **Audio:** all in code — 17 original songs (including a 3/4 \"Waltz of the Yikes\"), 7 short stings, about 45 sound effects, a generated anthem per nation, a crowd sound, and Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish voices for the commentators, the stadium announcer, the rival, the coach and the mascot.\n- **Online:** AI Hub ghost mode (\"Hub Heats\"); local play for 1–4 players.\n- **Testing:**\n  - Headless Node suites: core, all 12 events, the human input path, a scripted playthrough of the whole Games, attract, dev, local multiplayer, and a fake-hub suite.\n  - Playwright browser suites: the bundle from file://, touch-only, and the real AI Hub harness.\n  - All behind `node tests/run.mjs`; screenshots for look checks.\n- **Research:** none beyond the provided engine docs and the AI Hub README.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 21.\n   - 12 from the headless suites and simulations, for example:\n     - a weightlifting no-lift timer that never ran\n     - curling stones that never stopped\n     - an attract-mode infinite loop in figure skating\n     - an idle diver stuck on the dive menu\n     - a sprint HUD crash and a javelin crash\n     - ski-jump take-off and flight-angle bugs\n     - a downhill turbo that kept compounding speed\n   - The attract mode missing key presses during screen fades.\n   - 7 from screenshots, for example:\n     - the stands drawn over the track (+y points toward the camera in the 2.5D view)\n     - panels drawn magenta because of rgba() colours\n     - missing font glyphs\n     - the skier and the skater off screen\n2. **How many were critical / game breaking?** 6: the weightlifting hang, curling stones that never stopped, the attract loop, the stuck diver, the sprint HUD crash and the javelin crash.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the scripted playthrough of the whole Games, with the autopilot playing through real input. It finished in seconds and exposed the hangs, unqualifiable events and torch loops.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the \"perfect bot lands in the target band\" checks. They mostly tested my own tuning guesses against the plan's numbers.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **Liked most:** a multi-event sports game is easy to test without a browser: fixed-step `game.advance` and `input.tap`/`hold` for scripted play. The good-looking rigs, the road module for downhill and bobsled, the rhythm module for skating, and the free settings, pause and rebinding screens also saved a lot of work.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong:**\n   - The 2.5D view's +y direction: I assumed it points away from the camera; it points toward it.\n   - I passed rgba() colours where the engine needs hex.\n   - I assumed `music()` takes a start offset. It doesn't, so I moved the song clock myself to skip into a skating part.\n3. **Most important thing built:** the event kit:\n   - one event contract with a phase runner and race/attempts/match formats\n   - CPU attempts simulated off-screen\n   - the autoplayer that drives a human through real input\n   - the pose layer\n4. **Hardest part:** custom athletic poses. The rig's joint frame and limb lengths are only documented in the source, and whole-body rotation needed an engine change.\n5. **Changed in the engine** (all marked `// OLY:`):\n   - exported the engine's per-step hooks\n   - moved cosmetic randomness off `Math.random`\n   - added ✕ ○ □ △ and · — – ‹ › ✓ ↻ ↺ ● é to the fonts\n   - made PlayStation button symbols the default\n   - added a pose hook and body pitch/roll to the Humanoid\n   - added a voice volume bus\n6. **One change I'd make:** a documented pose/IK API on the Humanoid (named joints, `armTo`/`legTo`, body rotation), so custom sports animation needs no engine edits.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **Liked most:** it states concretely what \"done\" means — dev mode with sliders, attract mode, 10-foot controller-first UI, touch-only handling, the hub, a single file and one test command.\n2. **Confusing:** the plan carried another run's file names, and it wasn't clear how much of a 1,700-line plan is expected in one medium-effort session.\n3. **Hardest:** the breadth — twelve different mini-games, each with its own physics, art, bots, HUD and tuning — plus the ceremonies, campaign, attract mode, dev mode and the hub.\n4. **Would change:** allow fewer events for the demo, or rank the plan's features by priority, so time goes into depth and polish rather than breadth.\n\nThe development files (engine copy, source, tests, tools) are in `Olympiyikes-OlympicGames-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-074439/`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":942,"cache_read_input_tokens":716689,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":942},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d0d39672-91d7-4659-90e6-59a7cf2c42a6","uuid":"e86d24e0-9599-40d6-b454-5f924daf684c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T16:26:58.979Z","request_id":"req_011CgiCcHbepVU6UXfvv1sFK"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: Not all clauses are satisfied on transcript evidence. The bundle HTML and exit-interview.json exist and the final reply has the four sections. However, the condition requires every TASK.md feature to be implemented with no stubs or placeholders, and the transcript contradicts that: the reply says balance is untested by humans, the engine settings text is 'still on the small side at 1080p', and the TASK.md Testing section asked for a single command that prints only failures and a count, which the transcript shows only partly met. More importantly, the exit-interview.json fields testingFeedback, engineFeedback and promptFeedback were written as arrays, and the aiHub and sourceInformation.usage values are 'unknown' as allowed, but the transcript also shows the exit-interview.json was regenerated after the final feedback text was written, so its contents are not verified against the final reply. The 'usage' field is 'unknown' which is permitted. The clearest gap is that the reply itself states the work was cut short in places (balance untested, bobsled qualifying loosened, dive spin reduced), so the claim that every TASK.md feature is fully implemented is not supported."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d0d39672-91d7-4659-90e6-59a7cf2c42a6","uuid":"6f330251-bd9f-4261-a048-06c142bfccee","timestamp":"2026-11-04T16:27:05.824Z","isSynthetic":true}
